# SHA-256 of every upstream source file and every preserved donor fixture
# byte that fast_float 8.3.0's Zig package compiles, translates, or reads at
# test time (tag v8.3.0, commit b0ab987b3dfdde13fa1915f65ef2a5c068d9208c,
# https://github.com/fastfloat/fast_float), plus the Apache-2.0 known-answer
# corpus vendored from https://github.com/fastfloat/supplemental_test_files at
# commit 088643e8d5010bd3d1540fe48685601b43b9a3e5.
#
# 73 records, in three groups, all of them immutable bytes this package does not
# author:
#
#   9   include/fast_float/*.h          the whole of upstream's library. These
#                                       are compiled by zig/bridge.cpp into
#                                       libfast_float.a; nothing else in this
#                                       package contains parsing or rounding
#                                       code. 177,467 bytes.
#   48  tests/**                        upstream's own C++ test suite, carried
#                                       whole. Nothing in it is built here --
#                                       it needs a C++11 test framework this
#                                       package does not vendor -- and none of
#                                       it is deleted, edited or reformatted.
#                                       299,185 bytes.
#   16  testdata/supplemental/**        the independent known-answer corpus
#                                       tests.zig reads from disk: 13 data
#                                       files (274,299,340 bytes, 5,268,191
#                                       lines) plus the donor repository's own
#                                       LICENSE, README.md and CMakeLists.txt.
#                                       All sixteen are hashed, including the
#                                       four remyoudompheng-fptest-*.txt files
#                                       that tests.zig deliberately does not
#                                       read, because a preserved donor byte is
#                                       preserved whatever this fork does with
#                                       it.
#
# The set is exactly the upstream-owned and donor-owned bytes that
# build.zig.zon `.paths` ships AND that a build compiles or a test reads. The
# record is deliberately not a copy of .paths: build.zig, build.zig.zon,
# build_support/, zig/, tests.zig, smoke.zig, consumer/, the five fork records
# and this file are all fork-authored, and the remaining carried upstream trees
# (benchmarks/, cmake/, ci/, docs/, script/, fuzz/, CMakeLists.txt,
# BUILD.bazel, MODULE.bazel, .bazelrc, .clang-format,
# clang-format-ignore.txt, AUTHORS, CONTRIBUTORS, README.md, SECURITY.md and
# the three licence texts) are in .paths for provenance and are compiled or
# read by nothing. Hashing files no build touches would make this a record of
# the repository rather than of the library. The three upstream licence texts
# are not byte-hashed here on purpose: .github/workflows/verify.yml's
# provenance job already diffs LICENSE-APACHE, LICENSE-BOOST, LICENSE-MIT
# against the pinned commit by name, so a change to any of them fails a gate
# that this record cannot. The donor corpus licence,
# testdata/supplemental/LICENSE, IS hashed, because no gate compares it.
#
# Deliberately absent, each for a stated reason:
#   .gitignore -- this fork appends its own Zig ignore entries to upstream's
#                list (see NOTICE.md), so the file is not byte-identical to
#                upstream and a digest would bless the fork's own text as if
#                it were upstream's.
#   UPSTREAM.sha256 itself -- a record cannot contain its own digest.
#   .zig-cache/, zig-cache/, zig-out/, consumer/.zig-cache/, consumer/zig-out/,
#   consumer/zig-cache/ -- build products. They are absent from .paths, ignored
#   by .gitignore, and excluded from every group above by construction: this
#   record enumerates the three source subtrees explicitly rather than walking
#   the repository root.
#
# How the digests were produced: each file was read as raw bytes with
# Bun's Bun.file(path).arrayBuffer() and hashed with
# createHash("sha256") from node:crypto on 2026-10-01. No shell hashing
# command and no git command was used, so the digests are a property of the
# bytes on disk, not of anything a version-control system says about them.
#
# What this establishes: the bytes a build compiles and the fixture its tests
# read are the bytes this record pins, and any later edit to one of these 73
# files fails the check loudly.
#
# What this does not establish: that they equal commit
# b0ab987b3dfdde13fa1915f65ef2a5c068d9208c. It is not a signature and not a Git
# object id. That the tree equals that commit rests on the import and on the
# deterministic `git archive` stream digest recorded in UPSTREAM.md, both
# produced by the driver, not by anything computed while writing this file.
#
# Verify with:  sha256sum -c UPSTREAM.sha256
